{
  "id": "COMPOUND:14268 - PROTEIN:61",
  "verbalization": "Benzocaine is implicated in the relationship with the Insulin receptor through a chain of targets involving 4-(3-Pyridin-2-Yl-1h-Pyrazol-4-Yl)Quinoline. This compound is identified as a target of benzocaine, and its interaction with the Insulin receptor is established through multiple pathways. The first pathway links benzocaine to Tumor Susceptibility Gene 101 protein, which is then targeted by 4-(3-Pyridin-2-Yl-1h-Pyrazol-4-Yl)Quinoline, ultimately leading to the Insulin receptor. Similarly, benzocaine is also linked to Glutamine--fructose-6-phosphate aminotransferase [isomerizing], which is also targeted by the same quinoline compound, reinforcing the connection to the Insulin receptor. These findings suggest that benzocaine may act through a shared mechanism involving the quinoline compound, which in turn influences the Insulin receptor. The evidence supports a potential role for benzocaine in modulating insulin signaling pathways, though the exact mechanism of action remains to be fully elucidated.",
